course: dictionary
lesson: 13022
title: Fabrication: a lie or a making
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fabrication is a noun, and it has two meanings. The first is a lie, or a made-up story that is pretended to be true. The second is the making of something, especially from many parts, as in a factory. Context clues tell you which meaning fits.
explain: «The detective said the confession was a fabrication, because the thief had never even been to the town.» The clues are detective, confession and never been there. A confession that is not true must be a lie, so fabrication means a lie here.
explain: «The factory does steel fabrication, cutting and joining metal beams for new bridges.» The clues are factory, cutting and joining. Here fabrication means making something from many parts.
fact: Fabrication is a noun. Its plural is fabrications.
fact: «Fabrication» can mean a lie or a made-up story pretending to be true.
fact: «Fabrication» can also mean making something, especially from many parts in a factory.
fact: Phrases to know: «a complete fabrication», «pure fabrication», «steel fabrication».
fact: A good check is to swap in a simpler word, like lie or making, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The story about the talking dragon was a complete fabrication.» What does fabrication mean here? | A kind of sweet | A careful way of building a model castle | *A lie made up to seem true | A long walk home
exercise: «The factory specialises in steel fabrication, cutting and joining metal for cars.» What does fabrication mean here? | Telling a lie | A painting of a fish | *Making things from many parts | Losing something important
exercise: Which phrase means a total lie? | Metal beams | Steel fabrication | *Pure fabrication | Fabrication team at lunch
test: «Her excuse for being late was a fabrication; she had been watching television all morning.» What is a fabrication here? | *A made-up lie | A piece of metal | A long, boring book | A fast train
test: Tom says fabrication is only for bedtime stories. Is he right? | *No, it can also mean making things from many parts | Yes, it means a sleepy feeling | Yes, it only means a story for bed | No, it is a colour
exam: Which sentence uses fabrication to mean making things? | Her tale of the lost puppy was a fabrication | He told a fabrication about his homework | *The workers at the plant did steel fabrication all day | The reporter proved the story was a fabrication
